  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166863,
      "id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "233b862e-702e-50ba-9cc3-a59b7de2d9b5",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(4 x e^{4 x y})\\,i+(4 y e^{4 x y})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(2 x y + 4 y^{2})\\,i+(2 x^{2} + 4 x y)\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x + 2 y \\right)})\\,i+(- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x + 2 y \\right)})\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=4 x e^{4 x y}$ and $Q=4 y e^{4 x y}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=16 x^{2} e^{4 x y},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=16 y^{2} e^{4 x y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2 x y + 4 y^{2}$ and $Q=2 x^{2} + 4 x y$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=2 x + 8 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4 x + 4 y.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x + 2 y \\right)}$ and $Q=- 2 \\cos{\\left(2 x + 2 y \\right)}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=4 \\sin{\\left(2 x + 2 y \\right)},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=4 \\sin{\\left(2 x + 2 y \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
