  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166863,
      "id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "784d041a-ae24-50d0-8209-4a3a339e7561",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative in their respective domains?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(8 x + 16 y)\\,i+(- 16 x - 32 y)\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(- 2^{- 2 x + y + 1})\\,i+(2^{- 2 x + y})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(2 x \\log{\\left(y^{2} + 1 \\right)})\\,i+(\\frac{2 x^{2} y}{y^{2} + 1})\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=8 x + 16 y$ and $Q=- 16 x - 32 y$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=16,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=-16.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $F$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=- 2^{- 2 x + y + 1}$ and $Q=2^{- 2 x + y}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2^{- 2 x + y + 1} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 2 \\cdot 2^{- 2 x + y} \\log{\\left(2 \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=2 x \\log{\\left(y^{2} + 1 \\right)}$ and $Q=\\frac{2 x^{2} y}{y^{2} + 1}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{4 x y}{y^{2} + 1},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=\\frac{4 x y}{y^{2} + 1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8ff750da-93d1-50c5-87c2-5cf581454723",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
