  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166863,
      "id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "019fbcac-d3ec-519a-8d4e-bfcad54e28c9",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(- 8 x y)\\,i+(- 4 x^{2} - 8 y^{2})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(4 x - 4 y^{2})\\,i+(32 x y^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(\\sin{\\left(x y \\right)})\\,i+(\\cos{\\left(x y \\right)})\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=- 8 x y$ and $Q=- 4 x^{2} - 8 y^{2}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 8 x,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 8 x.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4 x - 4 y^{2}$ and $Q=32 x y^{2}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 8 y,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=32 y^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}$ and $Q=\\cos{\\left(x y \\right)}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=x \\cos{\\left(x y \\right)},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- y \\sin{\\left(x y \\right)}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
