  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166863,
      "id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "404ef1ec-1f3c-5ab1-a7e2-a209c9fe05f6",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(8 x e^{- 2 y})\\,i+(- 8 x^{2} e^{- 2 y})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(2 \\operatorname{atan}{\\left(y \\right)})\\,i+(- \\frac{2 x}{y^{2} + 1})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(8 x - 6 y)\\,i+(- 6 x + 16 y - 2)\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=8 x e^{- 2 y}$ and $Q=- 8 x^{2} e^{- 2 y}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 16 x e^{- 2 y},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 16 x e^{- 2 y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=2 \\operatorname{atan}{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q=- \\frac{2 x}{y^{2} + 1}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{2}{y^{2} + 1},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- \\frac{2}{y^{2} + 1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal everywhere, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=8 x - 6 y$ and $Q=- 6 x + 16 y - 2$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=-6,\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=-6.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
