  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166863,
      "id": "65adbd80-9647-59d8-bd4a-42f431587c85",
      "title": "Identifying Conservative Vector Fields"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8884347f-f059-522c-a0de-b563de549b35",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following vector fields are conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $F=(e^{x - 2 y} - \\sqrt{2})\\,i+(- 2 e^{x - 2 y} + 2 \\sqrt{7})\\,j$\n- **II.** $G=(- 24 x + 16 y^{3})\\,i+(48 x y^{2})\\,j$\n- **III.** $H=(\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1})\\,i+(\\log{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)})\\,j$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D=\\mathbb R^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D=\\mathbb R^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.\n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=e^{x - 2 y} - \\sqrt{2}$ and $Q=- 2 e^{x - 2 y} + 2 \\sqrt{7}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=- 2 e^{x - 2 y},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=- 2 e^{x - 2 y}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=- 24 x + 16 y^{3}$ and $Q=48 x y^{2}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=48 y^{2},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=48 y^{2}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $G$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\frac{2 x y}{x^{2} + 1}$ and $Q=\\log{\\left(x^{2} + 1 \\right)}$. Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}=\\frac{2 x}{x^{2} + 1},\\qquad \\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}=\\frac{2 x}{x^{2} + 1}.\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on $\\mathbb R^{2},$ we conclude that $H$ is conservative on $\\mathbb R^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "aa9b75db-633f-5e41-a248-db233bf9fcb9",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
